SlopOS: count the memory map after allocating its copy

SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
A modification of Limine (BSD-2-Clause, see COPYING in its tree). Applies to
the tree of the limine-12.9.1 release tarball with `patch -p1`;
scripts/ensure_limine.sh applies it and builds the loader SlopOS ships.

build_pagemap() and build_identity_map() take memmap_entries, then allocate
their copy of the memory map, then copy that many entries. The allocation is
carved from the top of a usable entry, and when the carved piece cannot merge
with a neighbour the entry splits and memmap grows by one; sorted by base, the
copy then leaves out the highest entry. On a laptop whose framebuffer sits
above every other region (0x4000000000) that entry is the framebuffer: it is
left out of the HHDM, and the kernel's first write to the screen faults before
it has an IDT. A carve adds at most one entry, so the copy is sized one larger
and the count is taken after the allocation.

Upstream status: reported as limine-bootloader/limine#687. The fix under
review, #688, is this change asking for memmap_entries + 2, which also covers
an entry running across 4 GiB, where one allocation adds two entries
(plans/bare-metal.md, Phase 7). The code is unchanged in 12.9.2 and on trunk
at bc8b95362de5 (2026-10-04). Drop this patch with the first release that
fixes it.

diff --git a/common/protos/limine.c b/common/protos/limine.c
index 34e1c16..6db73fd 100644
--- a/common/protos/limine.c
+++ b/common/protos/limine.c
@@ -109,9 +109,10 @@ static pagemap_t build_identity_map(void) {
 
     map_pages(pagemap, 0, 0, VMM_FLAG_WRITE, 0x100000000);
 
-    size_t _memmap_entries = memmap_entries;
+    // The allocation can split a memmap entry and so add one: count after it.
     struct memmap_entry *_memmap =
-        ext_mem_alloc_counted(_memmap_entries, sizeof(struct memmap_entry));
+        ext_mem_alloc_counted(memmap_entries + 1, sizeof(struct memmap_entry));
+    size_t _memmap_entries = memmap_entries;
     for (size_t i = 0; i < _memmap_entries; i++) {
         _memmap[i] = memmap[i];
     }
@@ -210,9 +211,10 @@ static pagemap_t build_pagemap(int base_revision,
         map_pages(pagemap, direct_map_offset, 0, VMM_FLAG_WRITE, 0x100000000);
     }
 
-    size_t _memmap_entries = memmap_entries;
+    // The allocation can split a memmap entry and so add one: count after it.
     struct memmap_entry *_memmap =
-        ext_mem_alloc_counted(_memmap_entries, sizeof(struct memmap_entry));
+        ext_mem_alloc_counted(memmap_entries + 1, sizeof(struct memmap_entry));
+    size_t _memmap_entries = memmap_entries;
     for (size_t i = 0; i < _memmap_entries; i++)
         _memmap[i] = memmap[i];
 
